 ## Background
-The Marching Cubes algorithm was introduced by Lorensen and Cline in 1987 [S1, S4]. The original formulation relied on a lookup table of 256 cases to determine triangulation. However, it was later identified that 8 of these cases (46, 47, 98, 99, 210, 213, 214, 215) are topologically ambiguous, leading to potential cracks in the mesh [Lookup Table and Ambiguity Handling](10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling.md). Nielson and Hamann proposed the Asymptotic Decider to resolve face ambiguities [S4]. Chernyaev later proposed Marching Cubes 33 (MC33) in 1995, which provides a complete table of 33 cases (32 geometrically distinct) that correctly represents all possible behaviors of the trilinear interpolant [S2]. Natarajan (1994) was the first to consider interior "tunnel" ambiguities [S2]. The algorithm is widely used in medical imaging (e.g., CT bone extraction) and scientific visualization [S3, unofficial].
